3bbffa8f9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83708/selection/759dd860f76d26cc35822a204ecbaa2a1fc75d2c4ba6a9c9ec3fdd6a12f61aa5" :answer-field/choices ["q-83708/selection/619a89b483f2961017d197f815a83dc7b075db343164f1de20214add90f34f13" "q-83708/selection/759dd860f76d26cc35822a204ecbaa2a1fc75d2c4ba6a9c9ec3fdd6a12f61aa5" "q-83708/selection/486fa942047e2c758d7a7b3876978f2478c4887280c7b4b5244952989b123478" "q-83708/selection/f98678a621a1dd7fb0a3e7224a98ec6903fc568e966d597a8a7ff8c6c7b8c5b1" "q-83708/selection/c4eded6f829e0be3f8980a5bbc6de9481056648d27aa59bf6972b52816e72e1b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83708" :question/id #uuid "5e22c72b-c450-5ccd-9f2e-57045a5d397d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83708" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83708/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83708"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710/selection/1ffedfea031146c785a8e6573fd72cdcb7ff681a740598546df2e079be27b2f7" :answer/id #uuid "cb971587-9dbc-502d-8136-05f596de11cf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710/selection/401cd87b0985ef42929e8b5323c5aff7ef7d4a20a5878d0c8609b05bcc16d27b" :answer/id #uuid "caafa56a-b0f9-5681-94d5-e65dea52de2f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710/selection/2f522a26191ed06cc29594151659e06689c967cda26b97601e91b0ce15fbeb93" :answer/id #uuid "1020ef86-7c42-5eaa-9590-185839669dd2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710/selection/b97222231d3f6ff9b3d89a8051afddfc52a908c25850b5827b61346b3a762cdc" :answer/id #uuid "83066b29-3058-5801-968f-15e6c02a41d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710/selection/d768eaf73e06bc1e2cb1b9a57eaf124179ae3f9a5e74c41193b4d3c10ce49e83" :answer/id #uuid "2038d76c-fff9-5bf5-a668-9a607f0f54c3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf3e8746-827f-5c31-85e6-e22e3e39d609" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83710/selection/b97222231d3f6ff9b3d89a8051afddfc52a908c25850b5827b61346b3a762cdc" :answer-field/choices ["q-83710/selection/1ffedfea031146c785a8e6573fd72cdcb7ff681a740598546df2e079be27b2f7" "q-83710/selection/401cd87b0985ef42929e8b5323c5aff7ef7d4a20a5878d0c8609b05bcc16d27b" "q-83710/selection/2f522a26191ed06cc29594151659e06689c967cda26b97601e91b0ce15fbeb93" "q-83710/selection/b97222231d3f6ff9b3d89a8051afddfc52a908c25850b5827b61346b3a762cdc" "q-83710/selection/d768eaf73e06bc1e2cb1b9a57eaf124179ae3f9a5e74c41193b4d3c10ce49e83"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83710" :question/id #uuid "c42e4814-ec50-5416-8161-bbb51b0ed62b" :question/math-academy-id "q-83710" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83710/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135/selection/9818904ff1208df7283e65043437ca13141203756ffd21b089828c6e2d4c4e28" :answer/id #uuid "774a9572-928c-59be-8851-b28589a4fade" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135/selection/dab63d54b8407aea530ca64bbf600d6cb3c845aa88f97c78eaf1049e07cdb913" :answer/id #uuid "98dec585-2908-558c-8fee-97e66f48adb5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135/selection/dfc32d25c692b0ab175f9ee643c8f699048b965bd8d2da0c255c3dc7d09db54b" :answer/id #uuid "9623059f-2139-53ad-a5d8-7bdf66cce09b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135/selection/6c3b3bb52f389acd5b986555da243b793e2b6f498428d62d515627c59bb79126" :answer/id #uuid "d4c97909-11ee-5c2a-9d1e-863b1ffc9516" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 4\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135/selection/a3600c5edd079a25c198d13cefbdbdb630ff9f19536544eceace780816b5d12e" :answer/id #uuid "a04e9141-6bc8-5ba3-a6b8-4e835b9616e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1997f22-fce2-54e9-8b3c-64e2c77673cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140135/selection/9818904ff1208df7283e65043437ca13141203756ffd21b089828c6e2d4c4e28" :answer-field/choices ["q-140135/selection/9818904ff1208df7283e65043437ca13141203756ffd21b089828c6e2d4c4e28" "q-140135/selection/dab63d54b8407aea530ca64bbf600d6cb3c845aa88f97c78eaf1049e07cdb913" "q-140135/selection/dfc32d25c692b0ab175f9ee643c8f699048b965bd8d2da0c255c3dc7d09db54b" "q-140135/selection/6c3b3bb52f389acd5b986555da243b793e2b6f498428d62d515627c59bb79126" "q-140135/selection/a3600c5edd079a25c198d13cefbdbdb630ff9f19536544eceace780816b5d12e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140135" :question/id #uuid "d5be1581-2907-53a1-96d1-84786f5058d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140135" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5$ are eigenvalues, and \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. Find the matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=A,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix." :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=4,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-140135/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140135"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592/selection/9e6f9ed3a5185ff983252e746427be662df78014f28a20c8b5b9dd7dc31b31ed" :answer/id #uuid "f265e444-ca24-5d51-865b-3160c158c937" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592/selection/d0b297f2aff381b785dbca9e4982295cd4879861866781046dd38cd4b7546dcf" :answer/id #uuid "2e5d6085-4ee9-5899-90ab-c3c762df42b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-3,$ and $c=0$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592/selection/ecfd77b3683692152e0b3cd3e1b581c8e392a9389bdd8f0132b2428a21eff8fe" :answer/id #uuid "5fdab30a-f558-5a60-9a4b-3a2e050665e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-1,$ $b=-4,$ and $c=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592/selection/ac3a3094da0e985b3c35de2fc9f156c844882d2e2bd6bf9957fc221fba00ca5c" :answer/id #uuid "e0f74798-4df0-53a0-a21e-354c2fd0aeb3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592/selection/42a6345953dd8141d1f450d896da2a9e03a7bedf44a45c95d6563efc5d7926bf" :answer/id #uuid "31149a85-3ec5-5052-b872-4fe4ea564c37" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=3,$ and $c=13$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "89d54674-7042-50b5-8943-de5e4a053921" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84592/selection/ac3a3094da0e985b3c35de2fc9f156c844882d2e2bd6bf9957fc221fba00ca5c" :answer-field/choices ["q-84592/selection/9e6f9ed3a5185ff983252e746427be662df78014f28a20c8b5b9dd7dc31b31ed" "q-84592/selection/d0b297f2aff381b785dbca9e4982295cd4879861866781046dd38cd4b7546dcf" "q-84592/selection/ecfd77b3683692152e0b3cd3e1b581c8e392a9389bdd8f0132b2428a21eff8fe" "q-84592/selection/ac3a3094da0e985b3c35de2fc9f156c844882d2e2bd6bf9957fc221fba00ca5c" "q-84592/selection/42a6345953dd8141d1f450d896da2a9e03a7bedf44a45c95d6563efc5d7926bf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84592" :question/id #uuid "ca9903e3-49ec-5aad-b531-9a0f3deac3f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84592" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ a & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & b\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal: \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 13\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=13,$ and $c=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-84592/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84592"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697/selection/e53a41dfa03729a3af5b229c55de9d55ce4ab986f2210666d5dc96371da1d35d" :answer/id #uuid "c68f4464-11fd-5336-a3f8-f66204bf5958" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697/selection/517c03841e5b3bd57232d98cbb1a2fcc855fecb678f4087ebaca77f8b6ee09c6" :answer/id #uuid "54ad46b3-b30c-525a-9ab2-194a63383c61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697/selection/ef8a27e195dd03a661d7a01e90aedd47cb0a6f7c0a7e4ceff149f90b4ea89fbe" :answer/id #uuid "ae852346-702f-5d88-ac37-5456474961af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697/selection/d84cb5e2743d69482a1441ea4180921ccc935def20239c51b3d54e780bbfd531" :answer/id #uuid "a491ce5a-af53-52f0-9d21-af6e3a8a8177" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=-2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697/selection/d94416938a25a2da3e8ad3645defc9249f7e51dcd74a5b511b953ef89e0a7b6b" :answer/id #uuid "676169f7-bc8e-5ba4-b577-afc0762481eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c89b4e1-f82d-55a3-a28f-763563e807ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83697/selection/d94416938a25a2da3e8ad3645defc9249f7e51dcd74a5b511b953ef89e0a7b6b" :answer-field/choices ["q-83697/selection/e53a41dfa03729a3af5b229c55de9d55ce4ab986f2210666d5dc96371da1d35d" "q-83697/selection/517c03841e5b3bd57232d98cbb1a2fcc855fecb678f4087ebaca77f8b6ee09c6" "q-83697/selection/ef8a27e195dd03a661d7a01e90aedd47cb0a6f7c0a7e4ceff149f90b4ea89fbe" "q-83697/selection/d84cb5e2743d69482a1441ea4180921ccc935def20239c51b3d54e780bbfd531" "q-83697/selection/d94416938a25a2da3e8ad3645defc9249f7e51dcd74a5b511b953ef89e0a7b6b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83697" :question/id #uuid "c4ebc0e5-899d-5377-baaa-a3d1247291a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-83697" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2$ are eigenvalues, and\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & c & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & b & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=2,$ $b=1,$ and $c=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-83697/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0db5cbe-3288-5c04-a7f4-55ff5dd93ad5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83697"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/selection/d41c569707208b4074f1b79adce4f658c6fc5d5535006b64e1fc034d4fb6c872" :answer/id #uuid "6cbcc85b-6686-5ce5-b7a3-a499d4898fb3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/selection/dc35eaf03889b7cdf01af0446f93965eb83a685a5eb832bbe904f9541c4e5aaa" :answer/id #uuid "cc668511-0ccf-5d3d-8a9d-9410056d96f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/selection/0d0e3c2720b7f2bee2f17ccfd4e5bf7fbaced5388e8f428c618eb75e5cdff84c" :answer/id #uuid "1c2a887a-5180-5578-a732-b60d7c26f19b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/selection/47b96760cfd6d2cbdf03244bd588d74dbf33ee1d071d44a0388bc9386a654f9c" :answer/id #uuid "15eb039c-88ca-52be-817b-9a7800c1f674" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/selection/c2178d24b49491af60edf51be738e40548663117057101768b83d8d8712eccf8" :answer/id #uuid "4c4e50f9-ac5f-56e2-98d7-d5910c559180" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac58bd82-672d-5030-a429-397627bdd937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84598/selection/47b96760cfd6d2cbdf03244bd588d74dbf33ee1d071d44a0388bc9386a654f9c" :answer-field/choices ["q-84598/selection/d41c569707208b4074f1b79adce4f658c6fc5d5535006b64e1fc034d4fb6c872" "q-84598/selection/dc35eaf03889b7cdf01af0446f93965eb83a685a5eb832bbe904f9541c4e5aaa" "q-84598/selection/0d0e3c2720b7f2bee2f17ccfd4e5bf7fbaced5388e8f428c618eb75e5cdff84c" "q-84598/selection/47b96760cfd6d2cbdf03244bd588d74dbf33ee1d071d44a0388bc9386a654f9c" "q-84598/selection/c2178d24b49491af60edf51be738e40548663117057101768b83d8d8712eccf8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84598" :question/id #uuid "2e3e5aa9-3c15-5f62-9549-6ee08ad4a6f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-84598" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 0 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-4-\\lambda )(2-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-84598/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84598"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845/selection/36bd9158c0f8cf898d7dcb33dbab616a8a6b5b8d892e2e93eff67d276244638d" :answer/id #uuid "50fa5f46-7e8b-5a65-8789-6b75b5f99baf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "04d44da6-62f0-5008-a143-ac77f90aa360" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845/selection/4adc95def5d4917d91a4f22d84463106c2f6e3131789e2927889f8cef7cd5714" :answer/id #uuid "0a543dce-9c93-50bf-819f-a410fbd48d44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845/selection/4ad97c150a519e3b248566080099d0d71cc0a50cf175deeec1bdac31a2f52e6e" :answer/id #uuid "350fe377-4cef-5f0c-b869-d171f7b67004" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845/selection/6216ea5e36af87f473a62b816502003291336ea8eaedb104ce7cb1646e2c4448" :answer/id #uuid "7a37c77e-31e9-554e-8d80-005d1b4cb980" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "8bd142a5-9995-5a44-9329-82fb63673c8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37845/selection/4adc95def5d4917d91a4f22d84463106c2f6e3131789e2927889f8cef7cd5714" :answer-field/choices ["q-37845/selection/36bd9158c0f8cf898d7dcb33dbab616a8a6b5b8d892e2e93eff67d276244638d" "q-37845/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37845/selection/4adc95def5d4917d91a4f22d84463106c2f6e3131789e2927889f8cef7cd5714" "q-37845/selection/4ad97c150a519e3b248566080099d0d71cc0a50cf175deeec1bdac31a2f52e6e" "q-37845/selection/6216ea5e36af87f473a62b816502003291336ea8eaedb104ce7cb1646e2c4448"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37845" :question/id #uuid "13072b88-31f9-5595-bc7b-1289a2913b39" :question/math-academy-id "q-37845" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 1 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable.  \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is \n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-37845/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37845"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599/selection/7e9b11826892c2401190454bd5a08ca0adf991092ceb7f6f8d128feb98837afb" :answer/id #uuid "1c383382-f770-51d7-a2d8-8d674df21526" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599/selection/93c88cfe74d3961890af8d9fd1deab7761a32dc8786fb8f26344829ae162f602" :answer/id #uuid "dc51c4b5-aedb-56e1-85df-8dd8183838f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "7c95747c-ed23-529f-a91c-e61d6cad7ad1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599/selection/3d29a7b7d53e85f199d4627dc8efec70ff688cbca600f6a2628fe2fb3b6b043d" :answer/id #uuid "101b3006-c077-5e3e-a5b6-02b33d1f8b37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599/selection/342de2d065e890027cd0efacbf8d9ed9eac10176792d48fdf1417cd987a21817" :answer/id #uuid "6a62156c-b968-57e8-8974-7c1b4ba6c367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "ead3919c-5224-5225-8659-554b0d913c97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84599/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer-field/choices ["q-84599/selection/7e9b11826892c2401190454bd5a08ca0adf991092ceb7f6f8d128feb98837afb" "q-84599/selection/93c88cfe74d3961890af8d9fd1deab7761a32dc8786fb8f26344829ae162f602" "q-84599/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-84599/selection/3d29a7b7d53e85f199d4627dc8efec70ff688cbca600f6a2628fe2fb3b6b043d" "q-84599/selection/342de2d065e890027cd0efacbf8d9ed9eac10176792d48fdf1417cd987a21817"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84599" :question/id #uuid "457f60a1-a9cc-5ba4-93d9-6cd5c043c941" :question/math-academy-id "q-84599" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4 & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & 3 \\\\ 6 & 4-\\lambda  & 9 \\\\ -7 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -7 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+21) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +11=0,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-84599/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84599"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716/selection/18ecce5062e85fb5fb355e7b6c69c36f3898b541ad1d3b3be37ae79fc0574900" :answer/id #uuid "17bd21c4-e230-5005-aa4d-047812372eab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716/selection/9999da28108dfd717eb9e101842020ac50eba102133b0b55c80268b307cd94ad" :answer/id #uuid "1adfc03e-2ab3-52fa-b45e-65c4c143514f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716/selection/2f2b77725316951594807229435496cfd93f3be59f645db62e799cb563dc2d8e" :answer/id #uuid "15466bec-31f6-58ca-8867-3e8cc7ba9695" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716/selection/92f918862f94fcdb9e1dd0727a6f3282d33ed1533cd8c4560b3f70dc40de2213" :answer/id #uuid "9571d7fa-ba93-5b7a-a6e6-2d7712ab3ee5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716/selection/f47e34678b17f809770b5ee8670a14a4a690f37bfa769d4532dbe8f56b95eecf" :answer/id #uuid "ab02b01b-8a30-5142-a9c7-7e3ab9accd72" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "52437799-2d54-5be8-b6d7-e19713a0836f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83716/selection/18ecce5062e85fb5fb355e7b6c69c36f3898b541ad1d3b3be37ae79fc0574900" :answer-field/choices ["q-83716/selection/18ecce5062e85fb5fb355e7b6c69c36f3898b541ad1d3b3be37ae79fc0574900" "q-83716/selection/9999da28108dfd717eb9e101842020ac50eba102133b0b55c80268b307cd94ad" "q-83716/selection/2f2b77725316951594807229435496cfd93f3be59f645db62e799cb563dc2d8e" "q-83716/selection/92f918862f94fcdb9e1dd0727a6f3282d33ed1533cd8c4560b3f70dc40de2213" "q-83716/selection/f47e34678b17f809770b5ee8670a14a4a690f37bfa769d4532dbe8f56b95eecf"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83716" :question/id #uuid "26a7c787-d58c-5868-9f35-380ca2074586" :question/math-academy-id "q-83716" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible matrix diagonal matrix is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-83716/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83716"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "92e3ab69-745b-5752-8bd0-4a810f38b1e0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256/selection/b63a66ecafedace426a48f74c108fee9a78e6ed82476d2eee18af6e929a205e1" :answer/id #uuid "54b556cc-0d8a-5677-a4c4-0f89f2babf64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256/selection/a11af93747d3c3d9c6266b67202716a303e8bf18a833ec3ed605ffe03ac14bce" :answer/id #uuid "06e39217-4094-5d3a-8f77-7e2ef7081c2f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256/selection/260fc223a5f375ac71334fa8eb5534d72895c1be15dc1a1c11af42cda5cdf6f8" :answer/id #uuid "0e9b0536-0884-56b6-8a24-e5bc5ce50a31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256/selection/7e9b11826892c2401190454bd5a08ca0adf991092ceb7f6f8d128feb98837afb" :answer/id #uuid "fe54ea33-88ff-5db8-9b67-7c448be76436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "43642fdb-7d7e-50e2-8768-f428591a44a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37256/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer-field/choices ["q-37256/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37256/selection/b63a66ecafedace426a48f74c108fee9a78e6ed82476d2eee18af6e929a205e1" "q-37256/selection/a11af93747d3c3d9c6266b67202716a303e8bf18a833ec3ed605ffe03ac14bce" "q-37256/selection/260fc223a5f375ac71334fa8eb5534d72895c1be15dc1a1c11af42cda5cdf6f8" "q-37256/selection/7e9b11826892c2401190454bd5a08ca0adf991092ceb7f6f8d128feb98837afb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37256" :question/id #uuid "874949a9-baa2-57d2-b0be-9f3951630221" :question/math-academy-id "q-37256" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nDetermine whether $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $R.$\n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 0 & -6 \\\\ 14 & 3-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & -6 \\\\ 3 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )((2-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )+18) & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the quadratic polynomial  $\\,{\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda +8\\,$ we have that the discriminant $D<0.$ So, it doesn't have real roots. \n\nTherefore, $A$ is not diagonalizable over $R.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37256/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "05cb6509-dc1c-53b7-a495-4cfec2abdfe0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37256"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607/selection/31c89b4dba4d023ece1c7f751afab9798d971d6a3618560f6e88c3a6b570cad9" :answer/id #uuid "616927ef-758e-59b3-9a1d-7e0eda25d3b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607/selection/dd34c95ab37a5854b9ae0f6ef01ca4458da3789328d977d7b263d022d53b5778" :answer/id #uuid "1a71692f-fb6b-5951-9492-a99105e72e7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607/selection/f93fe2408e06e67046434ece1da8164a461c9f450cda3d853abba8e92bdcc3b6" :answer/id #uuid "1ee9afda-ada5-5ac7-8ac3-58b336211770" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607/selection/cd0cb0d60987b753a655b0bfe48a04d4da4209f1f19e239d9811326808404ad2" :answer/id #uuid "e43a7b97-5207-55f0-9f7e-1414c06eac3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607/selection/2510a3241ddb528e10e21b59d7837aa92aa4a577bbc545fb6b7d9214aa86bd40" :answer/id #uuid "0d911831-d43b-5217-aa13-6c86953402f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "56727957-d5b4-5b1b-b866-033cd2dec2bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84607/selection/dd34c95ab37a5854b9ae0f6ef01ca4458da3789328d977d7b263d022d53b5778" :answer-field/choices ["q-84607/selection/31c89b4dba4d023ece1c7f751afab9798d971d6a3618560f6e88c3a6b570cad9" "q-84607/selection/dd34c95ab37a5854b9ae0f6ef01ca4458da3789328d977d7b263d022d53b5778" "q-84607/selection/f93fe2408e06e67046434ece1da8164a461c9f450cda3d853abba8e92bdcc3b6" "q-84607/selection/cd0cb0d60987b753a655b0bfe48a04d4da4209f1f19e239d9811326808404ad2" "q-84607/selection/2510a3241ddb528e10e21b59d7837aa92aa4a577bbc545fb6b7d9214aa86bd40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84607" :question/id #uuid "02b892c4-044a-53a5-940f-9a5ebb949669" :question/math-academy-id "q-84607" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & ∗ \\\\ 1 & 0 & ∗ \\\\ 0 & 0 & ∗\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the third column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-4)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{3}I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations whose augmented matrix $M$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above matrix is already  in row echelon form.  It has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable.  From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{4}(-2(\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3})-3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(-4{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =-{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=2,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-84607/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84607"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37250"] :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}∗ & 1 & 1 \\\\ ∗ & 0 & 0 \\\\ ∗ & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $D={P}^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & -1\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:=\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{1}{3} & \\frac{1}{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850/selection/27a7660421125a8fdc8b84bcfdd23945c9dcfc5d5ad2b18528e91bf2618fea9b" :answer/id #uuid "4f9e8c90-eba9-54ef-a6a6-4d6f96b0d94e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850/selection/2a7d80cb7df107a99ad6b42336996a6a19fd27fd937ede9078c0c4c5bba29d6f" :answer/id #uuid "9ab4520b-2eaf-5c06-8d0e-2afb29df456d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" :answer/id #uuid "250aeb94-26b3-5db7-a268-7dc6ced96fe3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850/selection/87a453a31414b6ce9384e05b82e3bafd903ea206aeffee13e0c7e5e8242e569a" :answer/id #uuid "69b57ea0-4219-5c9a-98d4-37348b530a78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850/selection/c16f7f3e4fb668806294a6a99b33272cce1bb80f34e521b3aef141966167d2cb" :answer/id #uuid "c6d48c24-4cfa-5597-ba88-e96d700bfdf9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "84823393-1d61-5298-af3b-2086aa6e6c75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37850/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" :answer-field/choices ["q-37850/selection/27a7660421125a8fdc8b84bcfdd23945c9dcfc5d5ad2b18528e91bf2618fea9b" "q-37850/selection/2a7d80cb7df107a99ad6b42336996a6a19fd27fd937ede9078c0c4c5bba29d6f" "q-37850/selection/0e3bdff03e951f0a388d8b8654d11436ca9d62823f0936e95aee0fe9e94cf8ad" "q-37850/selection/87a453a31414b6ce9384e05b82e3bafd903ea206aeffee13e0c7e5e8242e569a" "q-37850/selection/c16f7f3e4fb668806294a6a99b33272cce1bb80f34e521b3aef141966167d2cb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-37850" :question/id #uuid "11178a4a-447e-5ebe-b505-90599256ee8d" :question/math-academy-id "q-37850" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & ∗ & 0 \\\\ 4 & ∗ & -4 \\\\ -1 & ∗ & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the second column of  the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=15,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=5.$ \n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Computing $A-(-1)I,$ we get\nNow, we simply need to find an eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}15 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -5 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}16 & -4 & -16 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ -10 & 1 & 10 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=-\\frac{1}{16}{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 40 & -4 & -40 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-40{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{4} & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37850/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37850"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "8fcde8bd-593c-5b86-8dcf-7f9230291a22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "08ae4e5c-227e-5275-bf06-5285f1e8d8f8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "6fb14d8f-2fec-55ab-a724-1cdfe6f2ef7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" :answer/id #uuid "323f7789-4192-5666-8644-89b915896130" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97" :answer/id #uuid "9908dedc-e3c4-5014-a85f-cc5aed89572f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1985070-6324-54ee-bf3b-7b23efef3c1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140148/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-140148/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-140148/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-140148/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" "q-140148/selection/7845f5bf94d2cfe99f73682a34529955af46f8f5d6f59bb198a07cbc245060af" "q-140148/selection/0f0db3a18f1d79c4863315eefe754cc5306e04e7aeebf4414ffedb95d6f1bc97"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140148" :question/id #uuid "65bfb5b4-8e98-560e-a200-cd992b31b578" :question/math-academy-id "q-140148" :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 9 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 10\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & a \\\\ 1 & 0 & b\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $a+2b.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=8,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=9,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=10.$ \n\nSo, the third column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}=10.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{3}=10.$ Computing $A-10I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-10I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 9 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]-10[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -1 & -3 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}↔{R}_{3}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -8 & -1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}=-{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3}.$ \nHence, the general solution is \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}{x}_{3} \\\\ -{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=4,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a+2b=-4+2(4)=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-140148/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e0a6d3b3-be78-540d-bb10-47c7fff64001"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140148"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37273"] :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & a & 1 \\\\ 1 & b & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $\\frac{a}{b}.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=3.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3.$ Computing $A-(-3)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]-(-3)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{3}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{1}{1}=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261/selection/64b5b3b36c2e978cc2b62d33d6cf1c523a5339ff90bfc33c2e24e21fa67314cf" :answer/id #uuid "4f47a5e4-ffdb-53ab-bb47-6683fb558523" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "b0f97d95-f0ef-5e63-8469-c25e6f3f792f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261/selection/f5ca17426b19a22e1b26cb968d8d766b1c4b861cfa8b20cec6dad95eb05dcd71" :answer/id #uuid "26a290d5-25df-5f52-9824-623fb9d2ad79" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261/selection/826d20ff74fb99abd9669169bdd675c7e5d3e1cc4bef441687a101d513ba2005" :answer/id #uuid "8b46387f-ac66-5c72-be05-e17da61e810f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261/selection/18f2d6627dfded91506ba5ffd81f7f9e415e7664cd4e26b7e7eddd38175044fa" :answer/id #uuid "3577329c-4aff-5594-b32c-7bf70ae6ca34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -9\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "74c1661c-5ca1-53c6-887c-23fb7a81864c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-140261/selection/f5ca17426b19a22e1b26cb968d8d766b1c4b861cfa8b20cec6dad95eb05dcd71" :answer-field/choices ["q-140261/selection/64b5b3b36c2e978cc2b62d33d6cf1c523a5339ff90bfc33c2e24e21fa67314cf" "q-140261/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-140261/selection/f5ca17426b19a22e1b26cb968d8d766b1c4b861cfa8b20cec6dad95eb05dcd71" "q-140261/selection/826d20ff74fb99abd9669169bdd675c7e5d3e1cc4bef441687a101d513ba2005" "q-140261/selection/18f2d6627dfded91506ba5ffd81f7f9e415e7664cd4e26b7e7eddd38175044fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-140261" :question/id #uuid "4f5c4210-464b-5376-870a-440393a4aae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-140261" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 9 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=9.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=6,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=7,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-9I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-9I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-140261/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-140261"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854/selection/d24691d753d35d38b47e00046503486957219f251e28cb1e5383a8e8f2cb4c2d" :answer/id #uuid "eefe16c4-3883-58b8-83af-6e0c7e64a242" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854/selection/639e2a4292455463f87abee8804f86640dd93130c1ec0839c12c0877b0b24b33" :answer/id #uuid "beaabb85-6ba9-5104-9d7e-f9466f8bf536" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854/selection/f15b1b112b59aa5b002ffbc7492c06e531bdc52082e2e22c54ef69d1dedae57d" :answer/id #uuid "bf26e090-46b7-587a-9585-de999811fc44" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854/selection/4277883a69e3bcb252b5adf06129e6d0434e1a83fa631f2d871851d06ede8da3" :answer/id #uuid "10489e03-1a2f-5e7f-be30-f42fc8e7432d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854/selection/3358621d976667bb027f50d3830633ab3f67228d68305f918075dfd6a4d22694" :answer/id #uuid "0f9ab072-4481-59a2-805b-d89e9d1e6027" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0c76fb4-953c-5fa7-9671-d58c460b6ccf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-83854/selection/639e2a4292455463f87abee8804f86640dd93130c1ec0839c12c0877b0b24b33" :answer-field/choices ["q-83854/selection/d24691d753d35d38b47e00046503486957219f251e28cb1e5383a8e8f2cb4c2d" "q-83854/selection/639e2a4292455463f87abee8804f86640dd93130c1ec0839c12c0877b0b24b33" "q-83854/selection/f15b1b112b59aa5b002ffbc7492c06e531bdc52082e2e22c54ef69d1dedae57d" "q-83854/selection/4277883a69e3bcb252b5adf06129e6d0434e1a83fa631f2d871851d06ede8da3" "q-83854/selection/3358621d976667bb027f50d3830633ab3f67228d68305f918075dfd6a4d22694"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-83854" :question/id #uuid "303c15f5-3e83-55c0-a519-b60eaa51efba" :question/math-academy-id "q-83854" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -1 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & 0 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -10 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-83854/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-83854"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611/selection/8fd08eafb02964bbf470083e81e7a6a42001c773d5f245cc8196af6281fa5bdb" :answer/id #uuid "678818c9-bcfd-5034-843c-e5ed4c4c509b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "cbaea40f-757e-51df-8249-71cbdcdd8467" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611/selection/13f6fe1683afe4042f115c3b8b19aa0a027fd5cb747079bffd967302f5f89aad" :answer/id #uuid "16db17d4-55cd-57f9-be99-e7f4fa0092f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611/selection/1f4dbcfaf49cceb9677190c4ac2ec1d7e457ed065753023fe7743f4b219d5ca2" :answer/id #uuid "ca34b7df-105e-5308-ae97-d5fc5ee036f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611/selection/39424b5656c9d36d34c5353a34b6f78fec4c5a3f4105e5cfbc626310eafcbbe7" :answer/id #uuid "1a5fa978-9c85-5bcf-9c7c-4c122b743ed8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "dc6b7c6a-be14-571a-8189-e14896588016" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84611/selection/39424b5656c9d36d34c5353a34b6f78fec4c5a3f4105e5cfbc626310eafcbbe7" :answer-field/choices ["q-84611/selection/8fd08eafb02964bbf470083e81e7a6a42001c773d5f245cc8196af6281fa5bdb" "q-84611/selection/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-84611/selection/13f6fe1683afe4042f115c3b8b19aa0a027fd5cb747079bffd967302f5f89aad" "q-84611/selection/1f4dbcfaf49cceb9677190c4ac2ec1d7e457ed065753023fe7743f4b219d5ca2" "q-84611/selection/39424b5656c9d36d34c5353a34b6f78fec4c5a3f4105e5cfbc626310eafcbbe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84611" :question/id #uuid "37c618a1-4145-554c-81e2-16575bea3127" :question/math-academy-id "q-84611" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-84611/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84611"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186124966 :question/answer-fields 17592186122818] {:db/id "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/9095eaea1a755bb68d03f02b41d35a3c49612fae7842182e213ae3543ba51927" :answer/id #uuid "5e410ebc-731b-59ec-9e5b-4cc2324ec7ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" :answer/id #uuid "92509958-184a-56da-b4c5-b7ea76a94457" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/77b76f62f68ac8a5132a6bd68e4c546d0c5253dbbb1ae8f974f56879711a06e0" :answer/id #uuid "da4e0b31-3e56-55a1-b233-43ed465d374e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/7a1ff3f324f4881c81b042ff2d543bafb0d2889d1e1d89d620bb2f3523f23f38" :answer/id #uuid "37504856-d640-586b-a556-4544e3527300" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/d8cf46fa14910a57ea25cb889d8fca97c275be8a7c9ae6eb90e71cfd3e34f69e" :answer/id #uuid "cf92bd5b-334f-5928-ade5-7a949a88ff07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07" :answer-field/id #uuid "273a45ff-9de8-5a69-92a3-78de7b49d576" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/77b76f62f68ac8a5132a6bd68e4c546d0c5253dbbb1ae8f974f56879711a06e0" :answer-field/choices ["q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/9095eaea1a755bb68d03f02b41d35a3c49612fae7842182e213ae3543ba51927" "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/fde7c7e8c5c0e1280c4619c1897573fc8396a25055092e595f0052889d677717" "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/77b76f62f68ac8a5132a6bd68e4c546d0c5253dbbb1ae8f974f56879711a06e0" "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/7a1ff3f324f4881c81b042ff2d543bafb0d2889d1e1d89d620bb2f3523f23f38" "q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07/d8cf46fa14910a57ea25cb889d8fca97c275be8a7c9ae6eb90e71cfd3e34f69e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-37275"] :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $A=PD{P}^{-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=1.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we find the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 6 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-1)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-1)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 2 & 2 \\\\ -12 & 3 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives us the eigenverctor ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-37275/selection/ma-v1/cb3d64e398a88bd4e4498be2fa5aee20cd1aed56443461598076b15664501d07"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610/selection/36ac9c78b5fc72c9be2a1a6286d95e1a833c5756bc1d4a6be7a465b21e5713e7" :answer/id #uuid "e98a8ee1-9825-5ff7-925e-a4128263b80f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610/selection/10d1f730a51a4f47755029debad3a4ca54c5a0a48ba96360c660a93d1d6ee1ce" :answer/id #uuid "780be5d2-4524-529a-b96d-0010b7dbb2d8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610/selection/2caf8873a0b6230d1da4479e09489513e971f6209e4cc0bf9afd97773ef077b2" :answer/id #uuid "c80fa701-d311-5537-911e-963d3e556ffc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ is not diagonalizable" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610/selection/fa73357f3c0d926d24dd73e1a17ecd1f11817d0b3312dd937a1d292f35a7939a" :answer/id #uuid "68748cbd-0a67-5d58-9e80-ef44fe41ffb3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610/selection/f5e631a0ffc699b956bf26009ae78668a1ea5c152e9da7065f00bff208330989" :answer/id #uuid "e51f5c63-d013-59ab-8b27-542cb274b937" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nand\n$P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "460d148a-37ab-5403-9041-893348fa0617" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84610/selection/10d1f730a51a4f47755029debad3a4ca54c5a0a48ba96360c660a93d1d6ee1ce" :answer-field/choices ["q-84610/selection/36ac9c78b5fc72c9be2a1a6286d95e1a833c5756bc1d4a6be7a465b21e5713e7" "q-84610/selection/10d1f730a51a4f47755029debad3a4ca54c5a0a48ba96360c660a93d1d6ee1ce" "q-84610/selection/2caf8873a0b6230d1da4479e09489513e971f6209e4cc0bf9afd97773ef077b2" "q-84610/selection/fa73357f3c0d926d24dd73e1a17ecd1f11817d0b3312dd937a1d292f35a7939a" "q-84610/selection/f5e631a0ffc699b956bf26009ae78668a1ea5c152e9da7065f00bff208330989"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84610" :question/id #uuid "54cd0c24-1bc4-5d0f-9bf4-9dbaab64f62d" :question/math-academy-id "q-84610" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-5I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-3)I & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-3)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-84610/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d22e4bf-1d84-587b-8106-e27319ecbc95"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5504"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A$ for which $\\lambda_1=-1,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ $\\lambda_3=0$ are eigenvalues, and\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/28c1e8d1a466398336c357726d7f345a39e6ace73ab4b36147328b3f0a7313b9.png)\nare the respective eigenvectors. What are the values of $a,b,c$ given that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/5611ead246c3e5a26d991603a68f3326b1c381d44eddd116316ca7ea6427fdd8.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since the eigenvalues of the matrix are $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$ $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ $\\lambda_3=0,$ we construct the diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/65491fb3de59e45bffb4774aca31cc25f20f555f18ffafc8d56221c689520380.png)\n\nNow, to construct the matrix $P,$ we simply write the respective eigenvectors as columns: \n\n- the $1$st column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_1={\\color{blue}-1},$\n\n- the $2$nd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_2={\\color{red}1},$ and\n\n- the $3$rd column is the eigenvector corresponding to $\\lambda_3=0.$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/3798d5e0b3305070161e2df70796a20a8fcd1e11f11cecdc94d59db520582ca3.png)\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/1448624dbf0dbb227d5e2a3f082dbda389c521d905b1eee1aafe9a2226e25cfd.png)\n\nIn conclusion, we get $a=0,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=-1.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5505"] :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/62f202b2fdbe406e4785cc4acf7f2b9aaa09980bc894340716fbbe5600fb8ae9.png)\nDetermine whether $A=PDP^{-1}$ for some invertible matrix $P$ and a diagonal matrix $D,$ where $P$ and $D$ are both real. In the affirmative case, find a possible diagonal matrix $D.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to determine whether $D$ is diagonalizable over $\\mathbb R.$ \n\nWe start by computing the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )([{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +2]-12) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -10) & =0 \\\\ (4-\\lambda )(\\lambda -5)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues of $A$ are $\\lambda_1=4,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=-2.$ Since all of the eigenvalues are simple roots of the characteristic polynomial, $A$ is diagonalizable. \n\nWe construct a diagonal matrix $D$ by writing the eigenvalues in the main diagonal. Therefore, one possible matrix $D$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/2f943468de2124874c2d806db25c6ca91a2df9a74902af95220e4974e5ef483b.png)\n\nNote that the diagonal matrix $D$ is unique up to the ordering of the eigenvalues. Therefore, another possible  diagonal matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/6f73d40195a91530d761d966db886e9ad2d87d7fc1059a4112a0a2701ce6a72f.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-5506"] :question/problem "Consider the following matrices:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/7d992806d0d5b90b65030f8c0080f105f67fbb7b4d3d807c3a719307e0c31e40.png)\nGiven that $D=P^{-1}AP,$ find the first column of the matrix $P.$" :question/worked-solution "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are  \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=-2, \\quad$ $\\lambda_2=-1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=2.$ \n\nSo, the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-2.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to $\\lambda_1=-2.$ Computing $A-(-2)I,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 14\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -5 & 9 & 0 \\\\ -16 & -10 & 16 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{2}){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{8}{3}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{3} & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}){R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -4 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, $x_3$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain $x_2=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get $x_1=x_3.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/fdf2311a1010ed8e8f25623328171687a63ea5da80f6e18a1a8dfbff9e5fae42.png)\n\nTherefore, setting $x_3=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/34e8934a56885ec189cc1819b1336f5b52b7708a092233e3c20e0960218cad10.png)\n\nFinally, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/ec378af1062dda61e89d060d565ef70bf7593a723bd3cba78dfd86f0d027dc5d.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-7330"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/ca47231d1f55afd5641f3924cae2252af74467ba29adbbdb51832cbd4cf06663.png) \nThe matrix $A$ has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=2,$ $\\lambda_2=1,$ and $\\lambda_3=0.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D=P^{-1}AP.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\qquad$ $\\lambda_1=2,\\quad$ $\\lambda_2=1, \\quad$ $\\lambda_3=0.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/b88fd696293e4151a71ca12d25cb56821ba034e07a876777fa30d5d6f6b9c4c3.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/38941ad5615365437a1aee93f085b33d6a240c5a61ce8e0f0a10acb465de0f18.png)\n\n- For $\\lambda_3=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives us the eigenvector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/a3ea884b0f6ba11582eb72e6cffcc871a144e55c446aaa304e1c7f9face13c7b.png)\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069769/assets/cc87c4f07116bf301d115ffeb00e81f9cd25d21cb40f61fdbc310f86557a61b4.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
